problems.problem:
There are $n^2$ draughts on an $n\times n$ chessboard. Can they be rearranged so that any two draughts that were a knight's move apart are, after the rearrangement, only a king's move apart (that is, stand next to each other)? Consider two cases:
a) $n=3$;
b) $n=8$.

In Shvambrania there are $N$ cities, every two of which are joined by a road. The roads meet only in cities (there are no crossroads; one road is raised over another by an overpass). An evil wizard introduces one-way traffic on all the roads in such a way that if one can leave a city, then one cannot return to it. Prove that
a) the wizard can do this;
b) there is a city from which one can reach all the others, and there is a city from which one cannot leave;
c) there exists a path passing through all the cities, and it is unique.

Consider the $4(N-1)$ boundary cells of an $N\times N$ table. One has to write $4(N-1)$ consecutive integers (not necessarily positive) in these cells so that the sum of the numbers at the vertices of any rectangle with sides parallel to the diagonals of the table, including "degenerate" rectangles, i.e. diagonals, is one and the same number (for rectangles $4$ numbers are added, for diagonals $2$ numbers). Is this possible? Consider the cases:
a) $N=3$;
b) $N=4$;
c) $N=5$.

A square $ABCD$ and a circle $O$ intersect in eight points, so that four curvilinear triangles $AEF$, $BGH$, $CIJ$, $DKL$ are formed ($EF$, $GH$, $IJ$, $KL$ are arcs of the circle). Prove that
a) the sum of the lengths of the arcs $EF$ and $IJ$ equals the sum of the lengths of the arcs $GH$ and $KL$;
b) the sum of the perimeters of the curvilinear triangles $AEF$ and $CIJ$ equals the sum of the perimeters of the curvilinear triangles $BGH$ and $DKL$.

A convex $N$-gon is cut by non-intersecting diagonals into triangles. It is allowed to perform the following transformation (rearrangement): taking a pair of triangles $ABD$ and $BCD$ with a common side, replace them by the triangles $ABC$ and $ACD$. Let $P(N)$ be the smallest number of rearrangements by which any triangulation can be transformed into any other. Prove that
a) $P(N)\ge N-3$;
b) $P(N)\le 2N-7$;
c) $P(N)\le 2N-10$ for $N\ge 13$.

A positive integer $N$ is given. Consider triples $(a,b,c)$ of distinct positive integers such that $a+b+c=N$. Take the largest possible system of such triples in which no two triples have common elements. Denote the number of triples in this system by $K(N)$. Prove that
a) $K(N)>\frac{N}{6}-1$;
b) $K(N)<\frac{2N}{9}$.

There is a rectangular $M\times N$ board divided into $1\times 1$ cells. In addition, there are many dominoes of size $1\times 2$. The dominoes are laid on the board so that each domino occupies two cells. The board is not completely filled, but in such a way that it is impossible to move the dominoes (the board has edges, so the dominoes cannot go beyond the board). Prove that the number of uncovered cells is
a) less than $\frac14 MN$;
b) less than $\frac15 MN$.

$n$ different cards are stacked in a deck. It is allowed to move any number of adjacent cards (without changing their order and without turning them over) to another place in the deck. It is required to put all $n$ cards in the reverse order by several such operations.
a) Prove that for $n=9$ this can be done in $5$ operations.
Prove that for $n=52$
b) this can be done in $27$ operations;
c) this cannot be done in $17$ operations;
d) this cannot be done in $26$ operations.

Can positive integers be placed in a $4\times 4$ table so that the following conditions hold simultaneously:
1) the products of the numbers standing in one row are the same for all rows;
2) the products of the numbers standing in one column are the same for all columns;
3) there are no equal numbers among them;
4) all the numbers are at most $100$?

$n$ numbers ($n>1$) are called close if each of them is less than the sum of all the numbers divided by $n-1$. Let $a, b, c, \ldots$ be $n$ close numbers and $S$ their sum. Prove that
a) all of them are positive;
b) always $a+b>c$;
c) always $a+b\ge\frac{S}{n-1}$.

You have to place ships in a $10\times 10$ square of squared paper: one ship $1\times 4$, two ships $1\times 3$, three $1\times 2$ and four $1\times 1$. The ships must not have common points (not even vertices) with each other, but they may adjoin the boundary of the square. Prove that
a) if they are placed in the order indicated above (starting with the large ones), then the process can always be completed, even if at each moment you care only about the current ship without thinking about the future ones;
b) if they are placed in the reverse order (starting with the small ones), then a situation may arise in which the next ship cannot be placed (give an example).

Do there exist three different primes $p$, $q$, $r$ such that $p^2+d$ is divisible by $qr$, $q^2+d$ is divisible by $rp$, and $r^2+d$ is divisible by $pq$, if
a) $d=10$;
b) $d=11$?

A "matloto" card is a $6\times 6$ table of cells. A player marks $6$ cells and sends the card in an envelope. After that six losing cells are published in a newspaper. Prove that
a) one can fill in $9$ cards so that among them there will necessarily be a "winning" card, that is, one in which none of the losing cells is marked;
b) eight cards are not enough for this.

In an equilateral triangle $ABC$ a point $D$ is taken on the side $AB$ so that $AD=AB/n$. Prove that the sum of the $n-1$ angles under which the segment $AD$ is seen from the points dividing the side $BC$ into $n$ equal parts equals $30^\circ$:
a) for $n=3$;
b) for arbitrary $n$.

Numbers are placed in a $3\times 3$ square (in the first row $a, b, c$, in the second $d, e, f$, in the third $g, h, i$, in exactly this order). It is known that the square is magic: the sum of the numbers in each column, in each row and on each diagonal is the same. Prove that
a) $2(a+c+g+i)=b+d+f+h+4e$;
b) $2(a^3+c^3+g^3+i^3)=b^3+d^3+f^3+h^3+4e^3$.

A number $N$ is the product of two consecutive positive integers. Prove that
a) one can append two digits to this number on the right so that a perfect square is obtained;
b) if $N>12$, this can be done in a unique way.

From weights of $1$ g, $2$ g, $\ldots$, $N$ g one has to choose several (more than one) with total weight equal to the average weight of the remaining weights. Prove that
a) this can be done if $N+1$ is the square of an integer;
b) if this can be done, then $N+1$ is the square of an integer.

A spider web has the form of a grid of $100\times 100$ nodes (in other words, a grid of $99\times 99$ cells). A spider sits in one of its corners, and flies have stuck to the web at some $100$ nodes. In one move the spider can move to any node adjacent to it. Can the spider be sure to eat all the flies, spending at most
a) $2100$ moves;
b) $2000$ moves?

A $3\times 3\times 3$ cube is glued together from $1\times 1\times 1$ cubes. What is the largest number of small cubes that can be removed from it so that a figure with the following two properties remains:
• seen from the side of any face of the original cube, the figure looks like a $3\times 3$ square (looking perpendicularly to this face, we see no gap: $9$ cubes of the figure are visible);
• moving within the figure from cube to cube through their common face, one can get from any cube to any other?

A set consists of all integers from $1$ to $100$ except some $k$ integers. Is it always possible to choose $k$ distinct integers in this set so that their sum equals $100$ if
a) $k=9$;
b) $k=8$?

Prove that the sum of the lengths of any two medians in an arbitrary triangle is
a) not greater than $3P/4$, where $P$ is the perimeter of this triangle;
b) not less than $3p/4$, where $p$ is the semiperimeter of this triangle.

Several distinct real numbers are written on a blackboard. Peter wants to make an expression such that its values are exactly these numbers. To make such an expression, he may use any real numbers, brackets, and the usual signs $+$, $-$ and $\times$. He may also use a special sign $\pm$: computing the values of the resulting expression, he chooses the values $+$ or $-$ for every $\pm$ in all possible combinations. For instance, the expression $5\pm 1$ results in $\{4,6\}$, and $(2\pm 0.5)\pm 0.5$ results in $\{1,2,3\}$. Can Pete construct such an expression
a) if the numbers on the blackboard are $1$, $2$, $4$;
b) for any collection of $100$ distinct real numbers on a blackboard?

Each side of a certain polygon has the following property: on the line containing this side there lies at least one more vertex of the polygon. Can the number of vertices of this polygon
a) be at most nine;
b) be at most eight?

There are $100$ doors, each with its own key (which opens only this door). The doors are numbered $1$ through $100$, and so are the keys. It is known that the number of every key is either equal to the number of the door which it opens, or differs from it by $1$. In one attempt you can select any door and any key and check whether the chosen key opens the chosen door. Is it always possible to find out which key opens which door
a) in $99$ attempts;
b) in $75$ attempts;
c) in $74$ attempts?

A city is a rectangle divided into equal square blocks with a single building in each block. Each building has $5$ floors. The law of renovation allows you to choose two blocks with a common side which contain buildings at this moment, and to demolish the building which has fewer floors (or the same number of floors). After the demolition, the number of floors of the demolished building is added to the remaining building. What is the smallest possible number of buildings that can be left in the city by using the law of renovation, if the city consists of
a) $20\times 20$ blocks;
b) $50\times 90$ blocks?

$1\times 2$ dominoes are placed on an $8\times 8$ chessboard without overlapping. They may partially stick out from the chessboard, but the centre of each domino must be strictly inside the chessboard (not on its border). Place on the chessboard in such a way
a) at least $40$ dominoes;
b) at least $41$ dominoes;
c) more than $41$ dominoes.

Prove that
a) any integer of the form $3k-2$, where $k$ is an integer, is the sum of a square and two cubes of some integers;
b) any integer is the sum of a square and three cubes of some integers.

Let us call the number of factors in the prime decomposition of an integer $n>1$ the complexity of $n$. For example, the complexity of the numbers $4$ and $6$ is equal to $2$. Find all $n$ such that all integers between $n$ and $2n$ have complexity
a) not greater than the complexity of $n$;
b) less than the complexity of $n$.

There is a convex quadrilateral $ABCD$ such that no three of its sides can form a triangle. Prove that
a) one of its angles is not greater than $60^\circ$;
b) one of its angles is at least $120^\circ$.

Is it possible to inscribe an $N$-gon in a circle so that all the lengths of its sides are different and all its angles (in degrees) are integers, where
a) $N=19$;
b) $N=20$?

At the centre of each cell of a checkered rectangle $M$ there is a pointlike light bulb. All the light bulbs are initially switched off. In one turn it is allowed to choose a straight line not passing through any light bulb such that on one side of it all the bulbs are switched off, and to switch all of them on. In each turn at least one bulb should be switched on. The task is to switch on all the light bulbs using the largest possible number of turns. What is the maximum number of turns if
a) $M$ is a $21\times 21$ square;
b) $M$ is a $20\times 21$ rectangle?

It is known that among several banknotes of pairwise distinct face values (which are positive integers) there are exactly $N$ false ones. In a single check, the detector determines the sum of the face values of all the real banknotes in an arbitrary set selected by us. Prove that in $N$ checks all the false banknotes can be detected, if
a) $N=2$;
b) $N=3$.

Ten children have several bags of candies. The children begin to divide these candies among themselves. They take turns picking their shares of candies from each bag, and leave just after that. The size of a share is determined as follows: the current number of candies in the bag is divided by the number of remaining children (including the one taking the turn). If the remainder is non-zero, then the quotient is rounded down. Is it possible that all the children receive different numbers of candies if the total number of bags is
a) $8$;
b) $9$?

Alice and Bob are playing on a $100\times 100$ square table, each cell of which contains either a plus sign or a minus sign. They take turns crossing out rows and columns of the table: Alice on her turn can cross out any remaining row of the table; similarly, Bob on his turn can cross out any remaining column. They repeat this until only one cell remains. If that cell contains a plus sign, Alice wins; if a minus sign, Bob wins. Does there exist a table such that, no matter whether Alice or Bob moves first, there is a winning strategy for
a) the second player;
b) the first player?

Alice has $60$ cards, numbered $1$ through $60$; each card contains a real number. Bob does not know these numbers, but he can choose any $17$ numbers between $1$ and $60$ and ask Alice for the sum of the real numbers written on these cards. Can Bob determine the sum of the real numbers on all $60$ cards by asking
a) at most $30$ questions;
b) at most $20$ questions;
c) at most $10$ questions?

$2N$ real numbers are given. It is known that if they are arbitrarily divided into two groups of $N$ numbers each, then the products of the numbers of the two groups differ by at most $2$. Is it necessarily true that if we arbitrarily place these numbers around a circle, then there are two neighbouring numbers that differ by at most $2$, for
a) $N=50$;
b) $N=25$?

An equilateral triangle is dissected into white and black triangles. It is known that all the white triangles are right-angled and mutually congruent, and all the black triangles are isosceles and also mutually congruent. Is it necessarily true that
a) all the angles of the white triangles are multiples of $30^\circ$;
b) all the angles of the black triangles are multiples of $30^\circ$?

The hostess takes a piece of meat from the fridge; kittens gather around her. Each minute, the hostess cuts a part from the piece and feeds it to one of the kittens (of her choice). Each time, the cut part is in the same proportion to the current piece. At some moment, the hostess puts the rest of the meat back into the fridge. Can the hostess give the same total amount of meat to each kitten if
a) the number of kittens equals two;
b) the number of kittens equals three?

Several people took part in a school competition in three sports: swimming, cycling and running. Besides the usual ranking in each sport, additional awards were given in three categories:
"The best swimmer among the five best cyclists";
"The best cyclist among the five best runners";
"The best runner among the five best swimmers".
Kirill boasts that he won all three additional awards, although in none of the sports was he among the three best. What is the smallest number of people that could have taken part in the competition?
